  critique: `A Stoic emperor is still an emperor. Marcus persecuted Christians during his reign (the martyrdoms at Lyon in 177 happened under him, though how directly he was involved is debated). He fought long, bloody wars of pacification and planned new provinces beyond the Danube. The *Meditations* never questions slavery, empire or his own right to rule. Stoic acceptance of “your role” fits very comfortably with the role of absolute ruler.

He also chose Commodus as his heir, breaking the century-long practice of adopting the best candidate. It was a catastrophe. For a man so sure of his judgement about others, it is a striking failure, possibly a father’s blind spot.

As philosophy, the *Meditations* is not original, and it isn’t an argument; it is a set of exercises. Some find its constant talk of death and insignificance depressing rather than consoling. There is a real question whether reminding yourself that nothing matters on a cosmic scale makes you calmer or just detached. And the popular “Stoicism” sold online, as a toolkit for productivity and emotional control, tends to strip out his cosmic piety, his sense of duty to others, and his gentleness, leaving a kind of self-help machismo he would not recognise.`,
  start: `Read the *Meditations* in Gregory Hays’s translation (Modern Library, 2002), which is the most alive in modern English. Robin Hard (Oxford) is more literal and has good notes. Read a few pages at a time, not straight through. Start with Book II and Book V, then go back to Book I. For understanding, Pierre Hadot’s *The Inner Citadel* is the best book ever written about it, and Donald Robertson’s *How to Think Like a Roman Emperor* is the best accessible one.`,
